// @check
// @error-at 47:10 no field 'b' on Redecl
// @error-at 48:24 cannot assign boolean to 'bad' of type string
// THE DONE-SET'S KEY IS THE ASSERTION, and these two errors are what carry it.
//
// A redeclared type name overwrites `cUserTypes` in pass 0a, so BOTH declarations
// resolve into the LAST placeholder and the fill registry is what stops the second
// body being appended to the first's. The registry is keyed by ARENA INDEX, so the
// two declarations below name ONE arena entry, it fills exactly once, and `Redecl`
// denotes ONE declaration's shape — never the merge of both.
//
// The pin is in two halves, and it takes BOTH errors to carry them. It used to take
// only one: the first message read `no field 'b' on {a: boolean}` and that structural
// spelling carried both halves at once. Diagnostics now render a declared type BY ITS
// NAME, so that message says `Redecl` and displays neither half. The evidence did not
// weaken — it moved, and here is where each half now lives:
//
//   `b` ABSENT  — carried by the FIRST error EXISTING at all. The second declaration
//     did not also fill the shared placeholder. A registry keyed by DECLARATION
//     STATEMENT instead (the looser key: two statements, so two fills) merges the
//     bodies into `{a: boolean, b: string}`, `r.b` RESOLVES, this error disappears
//     entirely, and the file's verdict moves to an argument-mismatch at the
//     `use({ a: true })` call. Same for a registry that answers "not yet filled"
//     always. So a broken done-set does not change this message — it deletes it.
//
//   `boolean`, not `Flag` and not a one-member union WRAPPER — carried by the SECOND
//     error, which is why the `bad` line exists. `Flag` is declared BELOW the
//     declaration that names it, so resolving `Redecl`'s field has to demand `Flag`'s
//     fill out of source order (`forceDeclFill`) and `declaredTyOfName`'s transparency
//     collapse has to find its one member present. An unfilled `Flag` renders as the
//     wrapper here. A `string` target is deliberate: it is a type `boolean` does not
//     reach, so the mismatch fires and prints the field's resolved type verbatim.
//
// Both halves have to hold at once, which is why they share one file: making the fill
// order-independent is what introduced the second caller of `fillTypeDeclAt`, and the
// done-set is the only thing standing between that second caller and a double fill.
//
// The corpus cannot reach this on its own — no other case under tests/cases/ declares
// one type name twice, so every alternative key above is byte-identical across all of
// it and invisible without this file.

type Redecl = { a: Flag }
type Redecl = { b: string }
type Flag = boolean

function use(r: Redecl) {
  print(r.a)
  print(r.b)
  const bad: string = r.a
  print(bad)
}

use({ a: true })
